8. grep 

grep "Keyword" filename - used to find particular pattern or workds in a file.

eg : grep "hello" <filename> : it will show only those lines where word hello appears.


*NOTE : The safest and most universal method is to break the leading dash pattern by prefixing the filename with its relative path (./ for the current directory) or an absolute path.
his forces the shell to treat the argument as a path instead of a command option.Read a file: cat ./-file.txt
why  ./ - as sometimes file name starts with '-' or '/' which is a terminal command, so to tell the command that it is a filename we use ./*



*NOTE : To handle spaces in a filename when using the command line or terminal, you must enclose the filename in *quotation marks or escape each space with a backslash.*
Command-line interfaces treat spaces as "argument separators" (delimiters). If you type cat my report.txt, the system thinks you are looking for two separate files named my and report.txt
eg : cat "my report.txt"
or : cat "./my report.txt"*



